  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative interviews with a specific demographic of highly educated renters in a single Chinese city, which may not represent the experiences of other socioeconomic groups or regions.",
      "what": "Through qualitative interviews with highly educated renters in Nanjing, this study examines how China's homeownership ideology persists despite falling property values. It details how renters internalize policy discourses of rigid demand, separating the physical function of housing from its financial performance, while facing social surveillance and emotional penalties within an unregulated rental market.",
      "why": "The study reveals the specific social and psychological mechanisms that maintain the cultural necessity of homeownership in China, demonstrating that the pressure to buy persists through social networks and market vulnerabilities even when housing is no longer a reliable financial investment."
